[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Extracting Component Classes and Managing Code Duplication in Tailwind CSS"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Tailwind CSS encourages utility-first development, allowing you to build complex designs directly in your markup. However, as applications grow, you may find yourself repeating the exact same long string of utility classes for similar UI elements like buttons, cards, and input fields. This tutorial explores best practices for managing code duplication without abandoning the core philosophy of Tailwind."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The Problem with Code Duplication"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider a standard primary button styled entirely with utility classes. Repeating this block across dozens of files creates a maintenance bottleneck. If you decide to change the button padding or color theme, you would have to update every single instance manually."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"<button class="px-4 py-2 bg-indigo-600 text-white font-semibold rounded-lg shadow-md hover:bg-indigo-700 focus:outline-none focus:ring-2 focus:ring-indigo-500 focus:ring-offset-2">Click Me</button>"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Solution 1: Leveraging Framework Components"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"The most idiomatic way to handle repetition in Tailwind CSS is by abstracting the markup into components using your JavaScript framework of choice (React, Vue, Angular, Svelte, etc.) or templating engine. This keeps the utility classes centralized in one reusable file."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"export function PrimaryButton({ children, onClick }) {
  return (
    <button
      onClick={onClick}
      className="px-4 py-2 bg-indigo-600 text-white font-semibold rounded-lg shadow-md hover:bg-indigo-700 focus:outline-none focus:ring-2 focus:ring-indigo-500 focus:ring-offset-2"
    >
      {children}
    </button>
  );
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Solution 2: Using Tailwind's @apply Directive"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"If you are not using a component-driven framework, or if you need to style traditional HTML templates, you can extract utility classes into custom CSS classes using Tailwind's @apply directive in your main stylesheet."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"@layer components {
  .btn-primary {
    @apply px-4 py-2 bg-indigo-600 text-white font-semibold rounded-lg shadow-md hover:bg-indigo-700 focus:outline-none focus:ring-2 focus:ring-indigo-500 focus:ring-offset-2;
  }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once defined in your CSS, you can apply the custom class cleanly directly in your HTML without cluttering the markup."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"<button class="btn-primary">Click Me</button>"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices and Trade-offs"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Prefer component extraction (React/Vue components) over @apply because it keeps your logic and styling tightly coupled in your component layer."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use @apply sparingly for small, highly specific items like typography styles or rich text content areas where components are impractical."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid premature abstraction. Wait until you have repeated a pattern at least three times before extracting it into a component or class."}]
